## Feature #2 Increment 1 (spec §4.2) — structured `Scope` spawn + collect
## happy path. `Scope.open do |s| ... end` yields a scope; `s.spawn(fut)`
## enqueues a child future (a top-level `async def` value — NOT an async
## closure, which the lowering doesn't support) and records its raw handle
## PRIVATELY (walled off — `spawn` returns nil); `s.collect` SYNCHRONOUSLY
## pumps the thread-local scheduler until every child completes.
##
## Two children each print a sentinel as they complete; `collect` drives both
## to completion, so both sentinels print. Proves: scope construction, the
## private child-handle list, spawn-enqueue, and the sync pump-collect.
##
## v1 surface (current-lowering): collect is a SYNC method (async methods on
## classes are unlowered) and spawn takes a future VALUE (async closures are
## unlowered). Scope.open is therefore callable from sync `main`.

## A child future: a no-await `async def` whose body runs (and prints) when
## the scheduler polls it to completion.
async def child_a() -> Int
  puts "child a ran"
  1
end

async def child_b() -> Int
  puts "child b ran"
  2
end

def main
  puts "scope start"
  Scope.open do |s|
    s.spawn(child_a())
    s.spawn(child_b())
    s.collect
  end
  puts "scope done"
end
